use std
into meshhit.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with mh_ is exported). It is the fast first pass of the assembly interference check in Amni-CAD: given the triangle meshes of two placed solids, it decides whether the solids could overlap, so the slow exact solid intersection runs only for pairs that might touch. It must never answer 0 for solids that overlap: when in doubt it answers 1. Lengths are millimeters.

```amni
extern fn mh_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "mh_view_f64";
```

Module state. Growable f64 arrays: `TA` (9 per triangle of solid A: x, y, z of its three corners), `TB` (9 per triangle of solid B), `RS` (4 results). Growable i32 arrays: `GS` (grid cell starts), `GL` (triangle numbers of B listed per grid cell), `SB` (stamp per triangle of B). Reals, all starting at 0: `GX`, `GY`, `GZ` (grid origin), `GH` (grid cell size). Ints, all starting at 0: `NX`, `NY`, `NZ` (grid cells per axis).

`RS` holds the results of the last `mh_near`: 0 smallest triangle distance found among the candidate pairs (1e30 when there were none), 1 candidate triangle pairs checked, 2 which test answered (0 none, 1 the surfaces come within the pad, 2 solid B starts inside solid A, 3 solid A starts inside solid B), 3 unused.

Sizing helper. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `zi(xs, n)` does the same for an i32 growable array with n zeros.

The exported function `mh_views()` registers every array JavaScript reads or writes through mh_view_f64: 1 `TA`, 2 `TB`, 3 `RS`. It returns 0.

The exported function `mh_setup(na, nb)` (two i32 triangle counts) sizes `TA` to 9 times na zeros, `TB` to 9 times nb zeros and `RS` to 4 zeros with `zf`, calls `mh_views` and returns 0. JavaScript then fills `TA` and `TB` through their views.

Vector helpers on plain reals, each returning a real: `dot3(ax, ay, az, bx, by, bz)` is the dot product. `clamp01(t)` is t limited to the range 0 to 1.

`ptri(px, py, pz, t, k)` returns the distance from the point (px, py, pz) to triangle number k of the f64 array t (corners a, b, c at t[9k] onward), using the closest-point-on-triangle method from Ericson's Real-Time Collision Detection: with ab = b minus a, ac = c minus a, ap = p minus a, d1 = ab·ap, d2 = ac·ap; when d1 and d2 are both at most 0 the closest point is a. With bp = p minus b, d3 = ab·bp, d4 = ac·bp; when d3 is at least 0 and d4 at most d3 it is b. vc = d1 d4 minus d3 d2; when vc is at most 0, d1 at least 0 and d3 at most 0 it is a plus ab times d1 over (d1 minus d3). With cp = p minus c, d5 = ab·cp, d6 = ac·cp; when d6 is at least 0 and d5 at most d6 it is c. vb = d5 d2 minus d1 d6; when vb is at most 0, d2 at least 0 and d6 at most 0 it is a plus ac times d2 over (d2 minus d6). va = d3 d6 minus d5 d4; when va is at most 0, d4 minus d3 at least 0 and d5 minus d6 at least 0 it is b plus (c minus b) times (d4 minus d3) over ((d4 minus d3) plus (d5 minus d6)). Otherwise with den = 1 over (va plus vb plus vc), v = vb den and w = vc den it is a plus ab v plus ac w. A zero denominator anywhere counts as that region's corner. It returns the length of p minus the closest point.

`sseg(px, py, pz, qx, qy, qz, rx, ry, rz, sx, sy, sz)` returns the smallest distance between segment p to q and segment r to s, with Ericson's closest points of two segments: d1 = q minus p, d2 = s minus r, e = r to p (p minus r), a = d1·d1, ee = d2·d2, f = d2·e. When a and ee are both below 1e-18 the answer is the length of p minus r. When a is below 1e-18: sp = 0, tq = `clamp01`(f over ee). Otherwise c = d1·e; when ee is below 1e-18: tq = 0, sp = `clamp01`(minus c over a); otherwise b = d1·d2, den = a ee minus b b, sp = `clamp01`((b f minus c ee) over den) when den is not 0 else 0, tq = (b sp plus f) over ee, and when tq is below 0 then tq = 0 and sp = `clamp01`(minus c over a), when tq is above 1 then tq = 1 and sp = `clamp01`((b minus c) over a). It returns the length of (p plus d1 sp) minus (r plus d2 tq).

`segx(px, py, pz, qx, qy, qz, t, k, ray)` returns an i32: 1 when the segment from p to q crosses triangle number k of t, using Moller-Trumbore with direction d = q minus p, e1 = b minus a, e2 = c minus a, h = d cross e2, det = e1·h; when the absolute value of det is below 1e-14 it returns 0; f = 1 over det, s = p minus a, u = f (s·h), outside 0 to 1 returns 0; qv = s cross e1, v = f (d·qv), when v is below 0 or u plus v above 1 returns 0; tt = f (e2·qv). When ray is 1 it returns 1 when tt is above 1e-9 (a ray from p through q, any distance), otherwise 1 when tt is from 0 to 1 inclusive, else 0.

`tdist(k, j)` returns the distance between triangle k of `TA` and triangle j of `TB`: 0 when any of the three edges of either triangle crosses the other triangle (`segx` with ray 0); otherwise the smallest of the nine `sseg` distances between the edges of the two, the three `ptri` distances from the corners of triangle k of `TA` to triangle j of `TB`, and the three from the corners of triangle j of `TB` to triangle k of `TA`.

`inside(px, py, pz, t, n)` returns an i32: 1 when the point lies inside the closed mesh of n triangles in t. It counts the triangles crossed by the ray from p toward p plus (0.57735, 0.57741, 0.57729) (`segx` with ray 1 and q being that second point) and returns 1 when the count is odd.

`cell(v, o)` returns the i32 grid index of coordinate v along an axis starting at o: the floor of (v minus o) over `GH`, limited to 0 at the low end; callers limit the high end.

The exported function `mh_near(na, nb, pad)` (two i32 triangle counts and a real pad) returns an i32: 0 only when the two solids cannot overlap. It sets `RS` 0 to 1e30 and 1, 2 to 0. When na or nb is 0 it returns 1. It finds the box of `TB` (smallest and largest x, y, z of all corners) grown by pad on every side, and the box of `TA` likewise. When the boxes miss each other on some axis it returns 0. The grid covers the grown box of `TB`: origin `GX`, `GY`, `GZ` is its low corner; the starting cell size is the larger of 2 times pad and 1.5 times the average over the triangles of `TB` of the longest side of each triangle's own box; `NX`, `NY`, `NZ` are 1 plus the floor of each box extent over the cell size; while `NX` times `NY` times `NZ` (as reals) is above 2000000 the cell size doubles and the counts are recomputed. `GS` gets `zi` of cells plus 1 entries. Each triangle of `TB` covers the cells from `cell` of its smallest corner coordinate minus pad to `cell` of its largest plus pad on each axis, the high ends limited to `NX` minus 1, `NY` minus 1, `NZ` minus 1; the cell number is ix plus `NX` times (iy plus `NY` times iz). A first pass counts covered cells into `GS` at cell plus 1, a running sum turns `GS` into starts, `GL` gets `zi` of the total, and a second pass with a cursor copy of the starts writes each triangle number into `GL`. `SB` gets `zi`(nb). Then for each triangle k of `TA`: its own box grown by pad; when that box misses the grown box of `TB` skip it; otherwise loop over the cells it covers (same limits) and over the triangle numbers j listed in each cell; skip j when `SB`[j] equals k plus 1, else set `SB`[j] to k plus 1; skip j when the grown boxes of triangle k and triangle j miss each other; otherwise add 1 to `RS` 1, take d = `tdist`(k, j), keep the smallest d in `RS` 0, and when d is below pad set `RS` 2 to 1 and return 1. After all triangles: when `inside` of the first corner of `TB` against `TA` (na triangles) is 1, set `RS` 2 to 2 and return 1; when `inside` of the first corner of `TA` against `TB` (nb triangles) is 1, set `RS` 2 to 3 and return 1. Otherwise return 0.

`main()` returns 0.
